1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,486
This is a 53 minute flight.

2
00:00:02,486 --> 00:00:08,226
A short, routine hop from Newark to Buffalo, on a snowy Thursday night in February 2009.

3
00:00:08,226 --> 00:00:12,532
49 people are on board. None of them will see the runway.

4
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:17,534
The entire disaster happens in the last 27 seconds.

5
00:00:17,534 --> 00:00:22,691
This is the actual reconstruction the investigators built from the black boxes.

6
00:00:22,691 --> 00:00:26,988
Watch the instruments. The plane is about to stop flying.

7
00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:31,983
It falls out of the sky and lands on a house.

8
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:36,884
49 people on board, and one man asleep in his home below. 50 dead.

9
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:42,530
But this is not really a story about one bad night in a cockpit.

10
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:49,919
It is a story about how two exhausted, underpaid, poorly trained pilots ended up flying you through a snowstorm in the first place.

11
00:00:51,066 --> 00:00:56,934
I only made 15,800 dollars last year. I'm just lucky I have a husband that's working.

12
00:00:58,066 --> 00:01:01,177
When it was over, everyone blamed the ice.

13
00:01:01,177 --> 00:01:05,454
It was winter, the wings were icing up, it seemed obvious.

14
00:01:05,454 --> 00:01:08,175
The investigators found something far more uncomfortable.

15
00:01:09,299 --> 00:01:14,210
Too tired to fly. The crash of Colgan Air 3407.

16
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:19,695
Chapter 1. Continental Connection 3407.

17
00:01:20,833 --> 00:01:23,544
February 12, 2009. Newark airport.

18
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:33,847
Passengers board a Bombardier Dash 8 Q400, a modern 74 seat turboprop, painted in the colors of Continental Airlines.

19
00:01:33,847 --> 00:01:36,559
Their ticket says Continental Connection.

20
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:42,953
But Continental did not own this plane, employ these pilots, or train them.

21
00:01:42,953 --> 00:01:50,631
The flight was operated by a smaller, cheaper regional airline called Colgan Air, flying under the big airline's brand.

22
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:55,325
This is how a huge part of American flying works.

23
00:01:55,325 --> 00:02:03,509
You buy a ticket from a famous airline, and you are quietly handed to a low cost contractor you have never heard of.

24
00:02:03,509 --> 00:02:04,932
Same paint. Different world.

25
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:10,585
On board are 45 passengers, 2 flight attendants, and 2 pilots.

26
00:02:10,585 --> 00:02:17,980
Among them is a woman who lost her husband on September 11th, flying to Buffalo to honor him.

27
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:19,623
Ordinary people, going home.

28
00:02:20,766 --> 00:02:25,525
The weather into Buffalo is poor but ordinary for a northern winter.

29
00:02:25,525 --> 00:02:31,474
Snow, wind, and light icing. Nothing a modern airliner and a competent crew cannot handle.

30
00:02:31,474 --> 00:02:33,853
The trap was never the weather.

31
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,991
The airplane itself is not the problem. The Q400 is modern, fast, and barely a year old.

32
00:02:41,991 --> 00:02:46,515
In the story that follows, the machine does almost everything right.

33
00:02:46,515 --> 00:02:51,039
It is the people, and the system behind them, that fail.

34
00:02:52,166 --> 00:02:54,270
Here is the economics that matters.

35
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:59,180
Continental sells the ticket, then pays Colgan a fixed fee to fly the route.

36
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:02,687
Colgan makes its money by keeping costs below that fee.

37
00:03:02,687 --> 00:03:06,194
And the biggest cost it can squeeze is the crew.

38
00:03:07,333 --> 00:03:16,785
Among the 49 aboard is a woman who lost her husband in the World Trade Center, flying to Buffalo to fund a scholarship in his name.

39
00:03:16,785 --> 00:03:20,420
A newlywed. A musician. Ordinary people, an hour from home.

40
00:03:21,566 --> 00:03:24,570
Chapter 2. Two tired pilots.

41
00:03:25,699 --> 00:03:27,733
The captain is 47 years old.

42
00:03:27,733 --> 00:03:32,477
He lives in Florida but is based in Newark, over a thousand miles away.

43
00:03:32,477 --> 00:03:37,898
He is a commuting pilot, and the night before, he slept in the airline's crew room.

44
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:44,022
The crew room is a lounge with couches, not a place to rest before flying.

45
00:03:44,022 --> 00:03:47,680
Colgan's own chief pilot admitted it was not adequate for sleep.

46
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:52,669
Pilots used it anyway, because a real crash pad cost money they did not have.

47
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:59,387
The first officer is 24 years old. Sharp, well liked, a trained flight instructor.

48
00:03:59,387 --> 00:04:04,974
She had just moved to Seattle to be near family. Her base was Newark.

49
00:04:04,974 --> 00:04:08,166
She commuted across the entire country to fly.

50
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:18,878
The night before the crash, she caught a red eye cargo flight from Seattle, riding in a spare seat, then a second flight, arriving in Newark at 6:23 in the morning.

51
00:04:18,878 --> 00:04:21,968
She grabbed maybe 90 minutes of sleep on the way.

52
00:04:23,099 --> 00:04:27,253
Why not just rest in a hotel? Because of what she earned.

53
00:04:27,253 --> 00:04:30,021
On the cockpit recording, she says it herself.

54
00:04:31,166 --> 00:04:37,265
I only made 15,800 dollars last year. I'm just lucky I have a husband that's working.

55
00:04:38,399 --> 00:04:43,460
The captain earned around 60,000, and even he commuted to save money.

56
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:47,677
At those wages, junior pilots did not stay in hotels.

57
00:04:47,677 --> 00:04:53,158
They flew standby through the night and started their duty day already exhausted.

58
00:04:54,299 --> 00:04:59,524
And she was sick. She had a cold, and can be heard sniffling on the recording.

59
00:04:59,524 --> 00:05:04,095
Earlier that trip, she weighed calling in sick, and talked herself out of it.

60
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:10,053
If I call in sick now, I've got to put myself in a hotel until I feel better.

61
00:05:10,053 --> 00:05:10,857
I'm pretty tough.

62
00:05:11,999 --> 00:05:15,626
So here is the crew the passengers were trusting that night.

63
00:05:15,626 --> 00:05:17,934
A captain who slept on a couch.

64
00:05:17,934 --> 00:05:24,198
A first officer who crossed the country without sleep, sick, because rest was a luxury she could not afford.

65
00:05:25,333 --> 00:05:30,434
The captain's road to that cockpit was long and troubled.

66
00:05:30,434 --> 00:05:34,005
He earned his private licence in 1990.

67
00:05:34,005 --> 00:05:41,146
But over the years, he failed FAA check ride after check ride. Instrument. Commercial.

68
00:05:41,146 --> 00:05:43,697
Multi-engine. His airline transport licence.

69
00:05:44,833 --> 00:05:50,885
To build the hours airlines wanted, he paid a company for the privilege of flying as a first officer.

70
00:05:50,885 --> 00:05:56,301
The pay to fly pipeline that feeds cheap regional carriers with pilots who look qualified on paper.

71
00:05:57,433 --> 00:06:03,508
When Colgan's application asked if he had ever failed a check ride, he did not disclose them all.

72
00:06:03,508 --> 00:06:06,884
The airline hired him without ever seeing his full record.

73
00:06:06,884 --> 00:06:10,259
There was no law then that forced them to look.

74
00:06:11,399 --> 00:06:16,393
He had moved to this airplane only months before. Ground school in October.

75
00:06:16,393 --> 00:06:18,313
His check ride in November.

76
00:06:18,313 --> 00:06:23,690
On the night of the crash, he had just 111 hours flying the Q400.

77
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:25,610
He was still learning it.

78
00:06:26,733 --> 00:06:29,304
The first officer's problem was never skill.

79
00:06:29,304 --> 00:06:31,875
Colleagues described her as sharp and thorough.

80
00:06:31,875 --> 00:06:38,854
Her problem was the life the job forced on her, and the poverty wage that made rest a luxury.

81
00:06:39,999 --> 00:06:42,597
This was not two unlucky individuals.

82
00:06:42,597 --> 00:06:47,793
It was the ordinary condition of a junior regional pilot in 2009.

83
00:06:47,793 --> 00:06:54,288
Underpaid, over-commuting, chronically tired, and quietly terrified of losing the job by calling in sick.

84
00:06:55,433 --> 00:07:02,627
In the three days before the crash, the captain bounced between hotels and that crew room, his sleep chopped up by the schedule.

85
00:07:02,627 --> 00:07:07,319
On the morning of the accident, another pilot saw him asleep on a crew-room couch.

86
00:07:08,466 --> 00:07:14,203
The captain was proud of how fast he had reached the airlines through the pay to fly pipeline.

87
00:07:14,203 --> 00:07:18,027
He said so on the recording, unaware it would ever be heard.

88
00:07:19,166 --> 00:07:23,106
I wanted to get into the one twenty one world as fast as I could.

89
00:07:23,106 --> 00:07:25,207
And it really worked out well for me.

90
00:07:26,333 --> 00:07:32,500
Neither pilot did anything malicious. They were, by every account, decent, capable people.

91
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:40,566
That is what makes this so unsettling. The system did not need bad people to kill 50.

92
00:07:40,566 --> 00:07:43,887
It just needed tired, cheap, under-trained ones.

93
00:07:45,033 --> 00:07:48,899
Chapter 3. A conversation, instead of a checklist.

94
00:07:50,033 --> 00:07:56,252
The flight itself is uneventful. They climb to 16,000 feet. The autopilot flies.

95
00:07:56,252 --> 00:08:01,035
And for almost the entire flight, the two pilots talk.

96
00:08:01,035 --> 00:08:05,341
About their careers, their pay, the industry, the weather.

97
00:08:06,466 --> 00:08:23,205
Buffalo approach control takes over and clears the flight to descend.

98
00:08:23,205 --> 00:08:41,466
Routine radio calls on a routine night. This is the actual recording.

99
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:48,710
There is a federal rule for this. Below 10,000 feet, the cockpit must be sterile.

100
00:08:48,710 --> 00:08:52,377
No chatter, no distractions, only the business of flying.

101
00:08:52,377 --> 00:08:57,673
It exists because the last minutes of a flight are where people die.

102
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:03,628
But as the plane descends through 10,000 feet, the conversation does not stop.

103
00:09:03,628 --> 00:09:10,686
It keeps going, all the way down, into exactly the phase of flight the rule was written to protect.

104
00:09:11,833 --> 00:09:18,357
The first officer yawns. She mentions, more than once, that she does not feel well.

105
00:09:18,357 --> 00:09:23,576
The captain is relaxed and chatty. Nothing in the mood suggests danger.

106
00:09:23,576 --> 00:09:29,231
That is the quiet trap of fatigue. It never feels like an emergency.

107
00:09:30,366 --> 00:09:33,576
The sterile cockpit rule exists for exactly this.

108
00:09:33,576 --> 00:09:39,596
Not because chatter is rude, but because comfortable, capable crews stop cross checking each other.

109
00:09:39,596 --> 00:09:44,812
The two people who should be guarding the airspeed are guarding the conversation.

110
00:09:45,933 --> 00:09:48,441
Chapter 4. Lots of ice.

111
00:09:49,566 --> 00:09:54,228
As they descend into the clouds, ice begins to form on the airframe.

112
00:09:54,228 --> 00:09:57,455
The crew notice it, and it clearly unsettles them.

113
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:01,754
That's the most ice I've seen on the leading edges in a long time.

114
00:10:02,899 --> 00:10:05,566
The deicing systems are on and working.

115
00:10:05,566 --> 00:10:10,137
Ice on a Q400 in these amounts is a normal, manageable nuisance.

116
00:10:10,137 --> 00:10:14,709
But the ice does something more dangerous than weigh on the wings.

117
00:10:14,709 --> 00:10:16,613
It captures the crew's attention.

118
00:10:17,733 --> 00:10:21,735
The first officer, it turns out, is deeply uneasy about ice.

119
00:10:21,735 --> 00:10:27,921
She admits she had barely seen it before joining Colgan and being thrown into a northeastern winter.

120
00:10:29,066 --> 00:10:35,477
I'd never seen icing conditions. I'd never deiced. I'd never experienced any of that.

121
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:39,977
So both pilots are now primed to fear the ice.

122
00:10:39,977 --> 00:10:46,396
And while they watch the ice, the one number that will actually kill them, their airspeed, is quietly falling.

123
00:10:47,533 --> 00:10:51,166
There is a switch on the Q400 for flying in ice.

124
00:10:51,166 --> 00:10:59,422
Set for icing, it tells the airplane to sound its stall warning earlier, at a higher speed, because ice makes a wing give up sooner.

125
00:10:59,422 --> 00:11:01,404
The crew, correctly, had set it.

126
00:11:02,533 --> 00:11:06,912
That was the right choice for the ice. But it had a hidden edge.

127
00:11:06,912 --> 00:11:11,917
It meant the stick shaker would fire earlier than the pilots might instinctively feel it should.

128
00:11:11,917 --> 00:11:14,419
Their margin was even thinner than it looked.

129
00:11:15,566 --> 00:11:18,152
Chapter 5. The approach.

130
00:11:19,299 --> 00:11:41,699
Approach control turns the flight onto its final course and clears it down toward the airport.

131
00:11:41,699 --> 00:11:54,299
Again, this is the real recording from that night.

132
00:11:55,433 --> 00:12:01,478
The captain calls for the landing gear and the flaps. Standard steps for landing.

133
00:12:01,478 --> 00:12:05,796
To slow down, he pulls the engines back toward idle.

134
00:12:05,796 --> 00:12:10,114
The plane is heavy, dirty, and bleeding off speed fast.

135
00:12:11,233 --> 00:12:13,506
Watch the airspeed on the reconstruction.

136
00:12:13,506 --> 00:12:17,293
It is sliding down through 145 knots, and still dropping.

137
00:12:17,293 --> 00:12:23,354
On the captain's display, a red and black warning band is climbing up toward the needle.

138
00:12:24,499 --> 00:12:27,305
That band is the low speed cue.

139
00:12:27,305 --> 00:12:32,115
It is the airplane, quietly warning that it is getting dangerously slow.

140
00:12:32,115 --> 00:12:37,325
Neither pilot calls it out. Neither adds power. The conversation is still going.

141
00:12:38,466 --> 00:12:43,473
The autopilot is still flying the airplane, and it is quietly doing something dangerous.

142
00:12:43,473 --> 00:12:50,627
As the plane slows, it trims the nose higher and higher to hold altitude, hiding how much effort that takes.

143
00:12:50,627 --> 00:12:52,415
The pilots feel nothing wrong.

144
00:12:53,533 --> 00:12:56,420
This is the hidden knife in modern stalls.

145
00:12:56,420 --> 00:13:00,390
The automation absorbs the problem, until it runs out of authority.

146
00:13:00,390 --> 00:13:07,247
Then it clicks off and hands two humans a fully developed emergency, with almost no time to understand it.

147
00:13:08,366 --> 00:13:11,292
Chapter 6. Twenty-seven seconds.

148
00:13:12,433 --> 00:13:17,784
The captain begins slowing the airplane less than three miles from the final approach fix.

149
00:13:17,784 --> 00:13:20,282
He pulls the power almost to idle.

150
00:13:20,282 --> 00:13:25,990
On a night with ice, heavy and dirty, this is where the margins get dangerously thin.

151
00:13:27,133 --> 00:13:27,951
Gear down.

152
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:32,669
The airspeed is about 145 knots, and still falling.

153
00:13:32,669 --> 00:13:39,413
The low speed cue keeps climbing toward the needle. Not one word is said about the speed.

154
00:13:39,413 --> 00:13:44,173
Fifteen seconds of silence, and then the airplane runs out of patience.

155
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:52,101
At 22:16 and 27 seconds, the control column begins to shake violently in the captain's hands.

156
00:13:52,101 --> 00:13:53,376
The stick shaker.

157
00:13:53,376 --> 00:13:59,328
The airplane's last, loud warning that the wing is seconds from an aerodynamic stall.

158
00:14:00,466 --> 00:14:06,317
Every pilot is drilled on the recovery from their first lessons. Push the nose down.

159
00:14:06,317 --> 00:14:11,777
Add power. Let the wing fly again. It is simple, and it is universal.

160
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:14,801
The captain does the opposite.

161
00:14:14,801 --> 00:14:19,365
He pulls the control column back, toward himself, pitching the nose up.

162
00:14:19,365 --> 00:14:25,831
It is the single worst possible input. He is pulling the dying wing deeper into the stall.

163
00:14:26,966 --> 00:14:29,111
The airplane fights to save itself.

164
00:14:29,111 --> 00:14:34,474
A second system, the stick pusher, physically shoves the nose down to break the stall.

165
00:14:34,474 --> 00:14:39,479
The captain pulls back against it, overriding the one thing trying to save them.

166
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:41,735
I put the flaps up.

167
00:14:42,866 --> 00:14:51,944
In the chaos, the first officer retracts the flaps, no one asked for it, removing lift at the worst possible moment.

168
00:14:51,944 --> 00:14:56,267
The Q400 rolls violently, past 100 degrees, wings nearly vertical.

169
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:58,230
Gear up.

170
00:14:59,366 --> 00:15:04,439
The flight data recorder captured every input. The column moved aft, hard.

171
00:15:04,439 --> 00:15:07,397
The engines were slammed toward full power.

172
00:15:07,397 --> 00:15:13,315
But pulling back while powering up only drove the nose higher, into the stall.

173
00:15:14,433 --> 00:15:17,656
A stalled wing does not fly. It drops.

174
00:15:17,656 --> 00:15:22,490
The Q400 rolled hard left, then snapped past vertical to the right.

175
00:15:22,490 --> 00:15:28,936
On a normal night the controls would lift it. On a stalled wing, they barely bite.

176
00:15:28,936 --> 00:15:32,562
Everything the plane did was the opposite of instinct.

177
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:39,433
From the first shake of that control column to this moment is about 25 seconds.

178
00:15:39,433 --> 00:15:42,108
There was time. The recovery was simple.

179
00:15:42,108 --> 00:15:48,224
But at 2,000 feet on a dark night, confused and afraid, the training was not there.

180
00:15:49,366 --> 00:15:50,122
We're down.

181
00:15:51,266 --> 00:15:53,539
Chapter 7. Clarence Center.

182
00:15:54,666 --> 00:16:01,509
The airplane falls onto a single family home in the quiet suburb of Clarence Center, five miles from the runway.

183
00:16:01,509 --> 00:16:05,272
It is 10:17 at night. Most of the town is asleep.

184
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:12,032
Neighbors run into the street to a wall of fire, fed by a ruptured natural gas line.

185
00:16:12,032 --> 00:16:17,334
There is nothing anyone can do. In the home, a 61 year old man is killed.

186
00:16:17,334 --> 00:16:18,991
His wife and daughter escape.

187
00:16:20,133 --> 00:16:26,187
All 49 people on the airplane die. With the man on the ground, the toll is 50.

188
00:16:26,187 --> 00:16:30,817
It is the deadliest crash on American soil since the September 11th attacks.

189
00:16:31,966 --> 00:16:41,213
And it happened not in a storm, not with a broken plane, but on a routine approach, in a working aircraft, with two qualified pilots at the controls.

190
00:16:42,333 --> 00:16:47,801
Within hours, the quiet suburb of Clarence Center becomes the center of the aviation world.

191
00:16:47,801 --> 00:16:58,007
A team from the NTSB arrives to a debris field the size of a single house, and a question that seems, at first, to have an obvious answer.

192
00:16:58,007 --> 00:16:58,372
Ice.

193
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:04,596
There were no survivors on the airplane. Whole families. A newlywed couple.

194
00:17:04,596 --> 00:17:06,720
A widow of September 11th.

195
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:12,665
Fifty futures ended five miles short of a runway, on a routine Thursday night.

196
00:17:13,799 --> 00:17:16,229
Chapter 8. The red herring.

197
00:17:17,366 --> 00:17:22,312
In the first days, the story writes itself. Winter storm. Icing.

198
00:17:22,312 --> 00:17:26,359
A regional turboprop goes down on approach to Buffalo.

199
00:17:26,359 --> 00:17:32,204
The early headlines all point at the ice. It is the obvious answer.

200
00:17:33,333 --> 00:17:35,196
The crew themselves believed it.

201
00:17:35,196 --> 00:17:41,530
When the plane began to shake, the captain almost certainly thought the ice had stalled his wing.

202
00:17:41,530 --> 00:17:46,002
But when the investigators examined the data, the ice story fell apart.

203
00:17:47,133 --> 00:17:52,763
The National Transportation Safety Board found the icing was light, and the airplane was fully controllable.

204
00:17:52,763 --> 00:17:55,579
The ice did not bring this plane down.

205
00:17:55,579 --> 00:17:59,802
The wing stalled because the crew let it get far too slow.

206
00:18:00,933 --> 00:18:06,636
They even put other pilots in simulators, facing the same night, the same warning.

207
00:18:06,636 --> 00:18:11,525
Trained, rested crews recovered without drama. The difference was never the airplane.

208
00:18:11,525 --> 00:18:16,006
It was the state of the two people flying this one.

209
00:18:17,133 --> 00:18:19,355
The flight data recorder settled it.

210
00:18:19,355 --> 00:18:26,393
The wing stalled at an angle and speed consistent with an ordinary stall, one caused by flying too slowly.

211
00:18:26,393 --> 00:18:31,579
Had ice brought it down, the numbers would have looked different. They did not.

212
00:18:32,699 --> 00:18:42,142
There was even a dangerous idea among some pilots at the time, that a Q400 could suffer a tail stall, where the correct response is to pull back.

213
00:18:42,142 --> 00:18:47,537
That was not what happened here. This was a normal wing stall, and it was mishandled.

214
00:18:48,666 --> 00:18:52,695
The recorders are pulled from the wreckage, scorched but readable.

215
00:18:52,695 --> 00:18:57,932
Within days, the Safety Board has the full picture the crew never had.

216
00:18:57,932 --> 00:19:03,974
The speed, the inputs, the 27 seconds, laid out second by second on a graph.

217
00:19:05,099 --> 00:19:09,592
They ran it through simulators and a dedicated study of the stick shaker.

218
00:19:09,592 --> 00:19:15,468
Every test pointed the same way. The wing was flying fine until it was starved of speed.

219
00:19:15,468 --> 00:19:18,578
And the recovery was right there, for the taking.

220
00:19:19,699 --> 00:19:25,205
The ice was real. The fear of it was real. But the data was merciless.

221
00:19:25,205 --> 00:19:26,673
This was not weather.

222
00:19:26,673 --> 00:19:34,380
It was a working airplane, flown too slow, and a warning answered with the one response guaranteed to kill everyone aboard.

223
00:19:35,499 --> 00:19:38,190
Chapter 9. The real cause.

224
00:19:39,333 --> 00:19:41,492
The Safety Board's conclusion is blunt.

225
00:19:41,492 --> 00:19:50,487
The probable cause was the captain's inappropriate response to the stick shaker, which led to an aerodynamic stall from which the airplane did not recover.

226
00:19:51,633 --> 00:19:56,192
The contributing factors read like a list of everything quietly broken.

227
00:19:56,192 --> 00:20:01,580
The crew failed to monitor their airspeed. They broke the sterile cockpit rule.

228
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:04,067
And Colgan's own procedures were inadequate.

229
00:20:05,199 --> 00:20:12,194
But the deeper you dig, the less this looks like two bad pilots, and the more it looks like a system that set them up.

230
00:20:12,194 --> 00:20:13,872
Start with the captain's training record.

231
00:20:14,999 --> 00:20:19,460
Over his career, the captain had failed five FAA check rides. Five.

232
00:20:19,460 --> 00:20:24,292
And on his application to Colgan, he did not disclose several of them.

233
00:20:24,292 --> 00:20:28,008
The airline hired him without ever knowing his full record.

234
00:20:29,133 --> 00:20:35,953
He had just 111 hours flying this specific aircraft, and had never felt the stick pusher fire in real training.

235
00:20:35,953 --> 00:20:41,410
When it shoved the controls forward that night, it may have frightened him into fighting it.

236
00:20:42,533 --> 00:20:46,937
And both pilots were exhausted. A captain who slept on a couch.

237
00:20:46,937 --> 00:20:51,708
A first officer who crossed the country overnight, sick, on a poverty wage.

238
00:20:51,708 --> 00:20:57,214
Fatigue does to the brain what alcohol does. It was in that cockpit with them.

239
00:20:58,333 --> 00:21:04,817
None of these, alone, is the reason 50 people died. Together, they are.

240
00:21:04,817 --> 00:21:12,797
Every layer that should have caught this, hiring, training, rest, procedures, had been quietly worn thin.

241
00:21:12,797 --> 00:21:14,294
To save money.

242
00:21:15,433 --> 00:21:17,497
Return to how they got there.

243
00:21:17,497 --> 00:21:23,688
The first officer had crossed three time zones overnight, slept perhaps 90 minutes, and was fighting a cold.

244
00:21:23,688 --> 00:21:26,783
The captain had slept in a crew room chair.

245
00:21:26,783 --> 00:21:29,879
Neither was rested enough to safely drive a car.

246
00:21:30,999 --> 00:21:34,827
Fatigue does to the brain roughly what alcohol does.

247
00:21:34,827 --> 00:21:43,334
It slows recognition, narrows attention to a single fixation, and destroys judgment, in precisely the seconds when judgment is everything.

248
00:21:43,334 --> 00:21:47,587
The Board could not measure it. It named it anyway.

249
00:21:48,733 --> 00:21:50,055
Then the training gap.

250
00:21:50,055 --> 00:21:55,012
The captain had never actually felt a stick pusher fire on the Q400 in training.

251
00:21:55,012 --> 00:22:00,300
So when it shoved the controls forward that night, it may not have felt like salvation.

252
00:22:00,300 --> 00:22:02,944
It may have felt like the airplane breaking.

253
00:22:04,066 --> 00:22:06,090
And the airline shared the blame.

254
00:22:06,090 --> 00:22:11,149
The Board found Colgan's own procedures for managing speed on an icing approach were inadequate.

255
00:22:11,149 --> 00:22:15,534
The company had not given its crews the tools to get this right.

256
00:22:16,666 --> 00:22:18,099
Stack it all up.

257
00:22:18,099 --> 00:22:28,843
A pilot hired without his full record, barely trained on the type, exhausted, is handed a sudden emergency by the automation, and does the one thing that guarantees a crash.

258
00:22:28,843 --> 00:22:32,783
It is not one mistake. It is a stack of them.

259
00:22:33,933 --> 00:22:37,486
The Board debated it for hours in a public meeting.

260
00:22:37,486 --> 00:22:41,749
Their language was careful and clinical. But the conclusion was not soft.

261
00:22:41,749 --> 00:22:48,144
This was a survivable situation, turned fatal by the people flying, and the company that put them there.

262
00:22:49,266 --> 00:22:54,717
And here is the quiet horror of it. There was no villain.

263
00:22:54,717 --> 00:22:58,351
No sabotage, no malice, no single catastrophic failure.

264
00:22:58,351 --> 00:23:05,619
Just a chain of ordinary, money saving decisions that lined up, one night, into 50 deaths.

265
00:23:06,766 --> 00:23:09,352
Chapter 10. Flying cheap.

266
00:23:10,499 --> 00:23:15,539
This is where the story stops being about one crew, and becomes about an industry.

267
00:23:15,539 --> 00:23:20,578
A regional airline wins its contract from a major carrier by being the lowest bidder.

268
00:23:20,578 --> 00:23:24,945
And you bid lowest by paying pilots less, and training them for less.

269
00:23:26,066 --> 00:23:32,530
In 2009, a first officer at a regional could earn less than the manager of a fast food restaurant.

270
00:23:32,530 --> 00:23:34,572
The talented burned out or left.

271
00:23:34,572 --> 00:23:40,015
The ones who stayed were often the most desperate, and the least able to say no.

272
00:23:41,133 --> 00:23:44,842
The public believed they were flying Continental, United, Delta.

273
00:23:44,842 --> 00:23:52,261
Often they were flying a contractor competing to be the cheapest, on the backs of exhausted, underpaid crews.

274
00:23:52,261 --> 00:23:55,971
A documentary at the time called it flying cheap.

275
00:23:57,099 --> 00:24:02,015
The families of the 50 refused to accept it as just another tragedy.

276
00:24:02,015 --> 00:24:05,795
For years, they traveled to Washington. They testified. They lobbied.

277
00:24:05,795 --> 00:24:11,466
They came back again and again, carrying the photographs of the people they had lost.

278
00:24:12,599 --> 00:24:19,058
And they won reforms no crash before them had achieved. New rest rules to fight fatigue.

279
00:24:19,058 --> 00:24:25,112
Mandatory stall training. A database so no airline could ever hire a pilot blind again.

280
00:24:26,233 --> 00:24:27,643
And the headline change.

281
00:24:27,643 --> 00:24:35,755
To fly passengers for a US airline, a first officer would now need 1,500 hours of flight time, six times the old minimum.

282
00:24:35,755 --> 00:24:39,634
The rule that exists every time you board a plane today.

283
00:24:40,766 --> 00:24:49,800
It is one of the only times in modern aviation that grieving families, with no power and no money, forced the entire industry to change.

284
00:24:49,800 --> 00:24:53,775
Every safe landing you have taken since is partly their doing.

285
00:24:54,899 --> 00:25:01,247
In 2009, a first officer at a regional airline could earn less than the manager of a fast food restaurant.

286
00:25:01,247 --> 00:25:03,151
The talented burned out or left.

287
00:25:03,151 --> 00:25:09,815
The ones who stayed were often the most desperate to keep the job, and the least able to ever say no.

288
00:25:10,933 --> 00:25:15,414
A public television documentary at the time laid it bare, and called it flying cheap.

289
00:25:15,414 --> 00:25:22,584
The travelling public had no idea that the famous name on their ticket had almost nothing to do with the tired crew up front.

290
00:25:23,733 --> 00:25:27,150
The centerpiece reform was the 1,500 hour rule.

291
00:25:27,150 --> 00:25:33,983
Before Colgan, a US airline first officer needed as little as 250 hours of flight time.

292
00:25:33,983 --> 00:25:40,817
After, six times that. The industry fought it hard, and argues about it to this day.

293
00:25:41,966 --> 00:25:49,373
Critics say the rule worsened a pilot shortage, and that hours alone do not teach the judgment that was missing that night.

294
00:25:49,373 --> 00:25:54,087
Supporters point to the record since. And the record is hard to argue with.

295
00:25:55,233 --> 00:26:02,883
Colgan 3407 was the last crash of a US passenger airline to kill this many people for well over a decade.

296
00:26:02,883 --> 00:26:07,255
Whatever those grieving families forced through the halls of Congress, it held.

297
00:26:07,255 --> 00:26:08,712
The skies got safer.

298
00:26:09,833 --> 00:26:12,454
The reforms went further than flight hours.

299
00:26:12,454 --> 00:26:18,444
New rules capped how long pilots could be on duty and mandated real rest between trips.

300
00:26:18,444 --> 00:26:23,312
The exact fatigue that sat in that cockpit, finally written into federal law.

301
00:26:24,433 --> 00:26:32,451
And a national pilot records database, so that never again could an airline hire a captain without seeing every failed check ride in his past.

302
00:26:32,451 --> 00:26:35,979
The precise gap that had put this captain in this seat.

303
00:26:37,099 --> 00:26:40,248
Aviation safety is often called a tombstone industry.

304
00:26:40,248 --> 00:26:44,972
The rules are written in the aftermath, paid for in the dead.

305
00:26:44,972 --> 00:26:52,057
What is almost unheard of is for the families of the dead to pick up the pen themselves.

306
00:26:52,057 --> 00:26:54,418
The 3407 families did exactly that.

307
00:26:55,566 --> 00:27:04,809
50 people died on a routine 53 minute flight, because the system that put them in the air had cut every corner it could.

308
00:27:04,809 --> 00:27:09,045
Tired pilots. Cheap training. A warning ignored, and a warning fought.

309
00:27:10,166 --> 00:27:14,641
The ice was never the villain. The cold arithmetic of flying cheap was.

310
00:27:14,641 --> 00:27:19,805
Remember that the next time an airline tells you your safety is their highest priority.

311
00:27:20,933 --> 00:27:24,831
If this story got its hooks into you, subscribe to Black Box.

312
00:27:24,831 --> 00:27:31,003
We take the biggest disasters in history apart, piece by piece, to show you the decisions hiding inside them.

313
00:27:31,003 --> 00:27:33,602
Because the truth is always in the details.

